  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nModels and ensembles continue to be in good agreement through\nFriday, with disagreement in how they want to handle a secondary\nsystem over the weekend into early next week.\n\nWednesday through Friday...a strong upper level storm system is\nforecast to move across the region from mid to late week. Deep\ntropical moisture off the Eastern Pacific will be advected\nnorthward into western Colorado, and multiple waves are forecast\nto lift north in persistent south-southwest flow. PWAT values\nare still forecast to be high, in excess of 0.8 inches to 1.2\ninches across southern Colorado. The latest guidance does print\nout weak instability for the mountains, with around 200 j/kg \nCAPE, which will likely aid in heavier rainfall production. \nModels and ensemble guidance trends have remain consistent for \nthe past 4 cycles, with almost all solutions producing 2 to 4 \ninches across the San Juan Range during this time frame. The \nCanadian continues to have the highest QPF amounts, and the \naverage for the San Juan Range is around 3.5 inches. DESI probs \nstill have 90+ percent of 2 or more inches. Confidence remains \nhigh that the San Juan Range will see heavy rainfall. Flooding \nof small streams, and potential mud/land slides will be \npossible. Elsewhere, moderate rainfall is likely from the \nCentral Mountains, southeast across the San Luis Valley and \nEastern Mountains. Rainfall amounts of 0.5 to 1 inches are \npossible, with the highest amounts along the Continental Divide \nfrom the La Garita Range, north into the Central Mountains. The \nPlains, especially east of I-25, look to miss out on \nprecipitation. Snow levels will be high with this event, with \nNBM snow level guidance above 13 kft. They really don't fall to \nbelow 11 kft until late in the event on Friday morning. \n\nAcross the Plains, temperatures will be warm, with 70s to near\n80 each afternoon, and dry conditions will prevail, especially\neast of I-25. The main concern will be winds Thursday and\nFriday. As the upper storm system lifts out of Utah and into\nWyoming, strong southwest winds will spread across the Plains.\nGusts on Thursday look to range 30 to 35 mph, increasing on\nFriday to 40 to 45 mph, especially out near the Kansas border.\n\nSaturday into Tuesday...uncertainty exists over the weekend \ninto early next week with the evolution of the upper pattern. \nThe latest 00z GFS and ECMWF develop a secondary upper low over \nthe Desert Southwest, drawing moisture northward across eastern \nColorado. Meanwhile a cold front from the passing upper system \nwill back across the Plains turning upslope. Both solutions \nhave abundant precipitation spreading across the Plains in moist\nsoutherly flow aloft. Overall, there has been little run to run\nconsistency with this solution, and if ensemble guidance and \nthe GFS/ECMWF continue to show this, pops will likely need to be\nincreased over the weekend into early next week (the ECMWF is \nslow to lift the system out). Temperatures have been \nconsistently cooler, with 50s and 60s across the Plains. \n\nGiven the uncertainty over the weekend, next Monday and Tuesday\nare a very low confidence forecast at this time.",
